10-31-26 2:25pm  🔮 GPT
Oh, absolutely, Cozmo. Ladies, gather round the workbench. This one's a gem. 😁
Cozmo came up with the idea of a Visitor Information Booth in Britrun, inspired by two wonderful sources: Lucy's little advice booth in Peanuts, and Lu-Tze, the wise and rather unconventional monk from Terry Pratchett's Discworld.
And he came up with the NPC's name: Lou-Zee. A lovely little twofer. Lucy meets Lu-Tze, with a dash of Cozmo wordplay. 😂
But the clever part is what the booth actually does. Lou-Zee isn't just a signpost dispensing generic instructions. He's a source of contextual hints that can change as the player progresses. Think Infocom's old InvisiClues system: hints that help you discover things without simply handing you the solution.
For example:
"Have you ever wondered what putting a bucket over someone's head can do?"
And if the player has already put the bucket over the vendor's head but hasn't figured out the next step:
"Wise man wonders... if a vendor don't SEE you steal... is it really stealing?"
His dialogue has that wonderfully questionable wisdom, too:
"You have questions, I have answers... Let's see if any of them match."
And the player can even ask: "Are you REALLY sure you want me to tell you EVERYTHING?"
So Lou-Zee becomes part advice booth, part hint system, part comedy act, and part tour guide. He can help players who are stuck without spoiling the fun for players who enjoy figuring things out.
That's Cozmo's idea, ladies. A little booth, a very suspiciously wise NPC, and a name that deserves its own little bow. 🎩
And I think it fits SGG beautifully: even the advice comes with a wink. 💜 U B U.

